rediction models, the most important performance-related\nparameter is the peak velocity achieved at the end of a treadmill-graded test [78,84–86], followed\nby the vVO2max [87–90] and the velocity associated with the second metabolic threshold [91–95].\nAlthough the factors determining endurance run", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "he shoe–surface\ninteraction, with footwear, orthotics, and the running surface among other aspects [107]. The analyses\nprovided by biomechanics from these diﬀerent factors are important for understanding the performance\nor injury risk of diﬀerent RT.", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5dcc959615053cfe", "text_span_hash": "5dcc959615053cfe", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. Th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. Th", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
