ention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "so, RE deteriorated when\nRT was altered with this method by reducing stride length and vertical oscillations [121]. In another\nrecent study using the Pose®method for gait retraining in recreational runners, it was demonstrated\nthat injury-related bio", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
