ners is the knee [148], with the existence of some investigations indicating that knee injuries might\nrepresent half of all injuries in recreational runners [145]. Although the lower leg, the foot, and the\nthigh are other common body locations for running-related injuries, the frequency of injury", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "times is the use of a walk/run strategy [152]. However, the eﬀectiveness of this\napproach for injury risk reduction is yet to be determined.\nThe most important intrinsic risk factor for endurance running injury is the existence of a previous\ninjury,", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
