bility but might negatively impact endurance running economy [20]; therefore, stretching could be\nperformed to allow normal range of mobility during running.\nThe available evidence is insuﬃcient to determine whether total energy intake per day or\nmacronutrient and micronutrient proportions in the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "ntradictory regarding the assumption that better muscle ﬂexibility, especially\nin the lower extremities, reduces the risk of injury in endurance runners [168]. Muscle ﬂexibility was\nsuggested to neither improve nor decrease likelihood of endurance ru", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0011_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "85afa56fd6ada2cf", "text_span_hash": "85afa56fd6ada2cf", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ry are suﬃciently\nsupported by science [172].\nIn summary, the evidence indicates that several intrinsic (sex, running biomechanics, anatomy)\nand extrinsic factors (experience, mileage, training routines) might contribute to the risk of injury in\nrecr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "It is a common belief that shoe cushioning technology protects against running-related injuries, but\ncurrent evidence does not provide any support for the beneﬁcial eﬀect of increased shock absorption\nto reduce the likelihood of injury [164,165]. Recent evidence suggested that optimal shoe cushioning\nproperties might diﬀer depending on the athlete’s body mass, because greater shoe cushioning might\nonly beneﬁt lighter runners [166].\nThe use of stretching exercises has become a key part of athletic training for endurance runners\nbecause of athletes’ perceptions that it prevents injury and may enhance running performance [167].\nHowever, the evidence is contradictory regarding the assumption that better muscle ﬂexibility, especially\nin the lower extremities, reduces the risk of injury in endurance runners [168]. Muscle ﬂexibility was\nsuggested to neither improve nor decrease likelihood of endurance running injuries, although extreme\ncases outside the normal range of ﬂexibility might be problematic because of interference with the\nnormal pattern of running [169]. In addition, the use of chronic stretching is eﬀective to increase muscle\nﬂexibility but might negatively impact endurance running economy [20]; therefore, stretching could be\nperformed to allow normal range of mobility during running.\nThe available evidence is insuﬃcient to determine whether total energy intake per day or\nmacronutrient and micronutrient proportions in the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "ry are suﬃciently\nsupported by science [172].\nIn summary, the evidence indicates that several intrinsic (sex, running biomechanics, anatomy)\nand extrinsic factors (experience, mileage, training routines) might contribute to the risk of injury in\nrecr", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0011_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "992cddd9e575ee93", "text_span_hash": "992cddd9e575ee93", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to the risk of injury in\nrecreational runners. While the history of a previous injury of the same type and in the same location\nis an intrinsic and unmodiﬁable factor with great inﬂuence on risk of injury, other factors, such as\nappropriate running", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "It is a common belief that shoe cushioning technology protects against running-related injuries, but\ncurrent evidence does not provide any support for the beneﬁcial eﬀect of increased shock absorption\nto reduce the likelihood of injury [164,165]. Recent evidence suggested that optimal shoe cushioning\nproperties might diﬀer depending on the athlete’s body mass, because greater shoe cushioning might\nonly beneﬁt lighter runners [166].\nThe use of stretching exercises has become a key part of athletic training for endurance runners\nbecause of athletes’ perceptions that it prevents injury and may enhance running performance [167].\nHowever, the evidence is contradictory regarding the assumption that better muscle ﬂexibility, especially\nin the lower extremities, reduces the risk of injury in endurance runners [168]. Muscle ﬂexibility was\nsuggested to neither improve nor decrease likelihood of endurance running injuries, although extreme\ncases outside the normal range of ﬂexibility might be problematic because of interference with the\nnormal pattern of running [169]. In addition, the use of chronic stretching is eﬀective to increase muscle\nﬂexibility but might negatively impact endurance running economy [20]; therefore, stretching could be\nperformed to allow normal range of mobility during running.\nThe available evidence is insuﬃcient to determine whether total energy intake per day or\nmacronutrient and micronutrient proportions in the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "to the risk of injury in\nrecreational runners. While the history of a previous injury of the same type and in the same location\nis an intrinsic and unmodiﬁable factor with great inﬂuence on risk of injury, other factors, such as\nappropriate running", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0011_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9adeca6d2630ccef", "text_span_hash": "9adeca6d2630ccef", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ion for long-distance events, such as marathons and ultra-endurance races, because running\nmileage should be balanced to ensure conditioning without increasing the risk of overuse injuries.\nRunners with one or more risk factors for endurance running", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "It is a common belief that shoe cushioning technology protects against running-related injuries, but\ncurrent evidence does not provide any support for the beneﬁcial eﬀect of increased shock absorption\nto reduce the likelihood of injury [164,165]. Recent evidence suggested that optimal shoe cushioning\nproperties might diﬀer depending on the athlete’s body mass, because greater shoe cushioning might\nonly beneﬁt lighter runners [166].\nThe use of stretching exercises has become a key part of athletic training for endurance runners\nbecause of athletes’ perceptions that it prevents injury and may enhance running performance [167].\nHowever, the evidence is contradictory regarding the assumption that better muscle ﬂexibility, especially\nin the lower extremities, reduces the risk of injury in endurance runners [168]. Muscle ﬂexibility was\nsuggested to neither improve nor decrease likelihood of endurance running injuries, although extreme\ncases outside the normal range of ﬂexibility might be problematic because of interference with the\nnormal pattern of running [169]. In addition, the use of chronic stretching is eﬀective to increase muscle\nﬂexibility but might negatively impact endurance running economy [20]; therefore, stretching could be\nperformed to allow normal range of mobility during running.\nThe available evidence is insuﬃcient to determine whether total energy intake per day or\nmacronutrient and micronutrient proportions in the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "ion for long-distance events, such as marathons and ultra-endurance races, because running\nmileage should be balanced to ensure conditioning without increasing the risk of overuse injuries.\nRunners with one or more risk factors for endurance running", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0011_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "08172c2b71e3adf8", "text_span_hash": "08172c2b71e3adf8", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "factors for endurance running injuries should be more aware of the ﬁrst\nsigns of injury and should immediately change their training practices or cease training to elude other\nIn this narrative review, we have presented a balanced overview, based on", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "It is a common belief that shoe cushioning technology protects against running-related injuries, but\ncurrent evidence does not provide any support for the beneﬁcial eﬀect of increased shock absorption\nto reduce the likelihood of injury [164,165]. Recent evidence suggested that optimal shoe cushioning\nproperties might diﬀer depending on the athlete’s body mass, because greater shoe cushioning might\nonly beneﬁt lighter runners [166].\nThe use of stretching exercises has become a key part of athletic training for endurance runners\nbecause of athletes’ perceptions that it prevents injury and may enhance running performance [167].\nHowever, the evidence is contradictory regarding the assumption that better muscle ﬂexibility, especially\nin the lower extremities, reduces the risk of injury in endurance runners [168]. Muscle ﬂexibility was\nsuggested to neither improve nor decrease likelihood of endurance running injuries, although extreme\ncases outside the normal range of ﬂexibility might be problematic because of interference with the\nnormal pattern of running [169]. In addition, the use of chronic stretching is eﬀective to increase muscle\nﬂexibility but might negatively impact endurance running economy [20]; therefore, stretching could be\nperformed to allow normal range of mobility during running.\nThe available evidence is insuﬃcient to determine whether total energy intake per day or\nmacronutrient and micronutrient proportions in the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "factors for endurance running injuries should be more aware of the ﬁrst\nsigns of injury and should immediately change their training practices or cease training to elude other\nIn this narrative review, we have presented a balanced overview, based on", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
