{"chunk_id": "2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running_p0008_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_093dfe9f5873", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cfb56b4447ee46c2", "text_span_hash": "cfb56b4447ee46c2", "source_file": "2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "romatosis, iron toxicity\naWorld Health Organization (2007)\nbSinclair and Hinton (2005)\ncZourdos et al. (2015)\ndMettler and Zimmermann (2010)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The role of dietary antioxidants for marathon runners and other endurance athletes\nremains only poorly understood. It is well documented that due to their increased\noxygen turnover, endurance athletes are exposed to greater levels of oxidative\nstress, and that supplementation of dietary antioxidants, primarily vitamin C and\nvitamin E, can attenuate measures of oxidative stress (Gomez-Cabrera et al. 2015).\nHowever, recent evidence suggests that the generation of oxidative stress per se is\nnot critical and in fact may trigger cellular mechanism that are required for optimal\ntraining responses to aerobic exercise training. Because supplementation of high\ndoses of dietary antioxidants may have the capacity to attenuate the beneﬁcial\neffects of chronic exercise training (Gomez-Cabrera et al. 2015; Braakhuis 2012),\nantioxidant supplementation is not recommended for marathon runners and other\nendurance athletes. However, consumption of antioxidants in the range of the\nrecommended daily allowance from food sources should provide health beneﬁts\nwithout impairing training responses, and should be highly encouraged in athletes\n(Pingitore et al. 2015; Braakhuis 2012).\nTable 3.1 Stages of Iron status abnormalities, their effects on marathon-related performance, and\ntheir prevalence in marathon runners\nPerformance and health effectsc\nUnclear, but supplementation\nImpaired marathon performance,\nimmunosuppression, reduced bone\nstrength; revers", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "text": "romatosis, iron toxicity\naWorld Health Organization (2007)\nbSinclair and Hinton (2005)\ncZourdos et al. (2015)\ndMettler and Zimmermann (2010)", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
