utic exercises for managing AT\n[145], specifying exercises based on muscle contraction type may be less important than\npatient education about mechanical loading and the prescription of exercises according to\nwhat the patient is most likely to perform [36]. Adherence rates and patient satisfaction\nwith mixed protocols appear to be at least as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "text": "universally prescribing eccentric exercises for tendinopathy, Millar et\nal. [36] suggest that clinicians should instead focus on conveying the principles of tendon\nloading and individually tailor the program to promote patient engagement with and con", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
