eutic exercises for managing AT\n[145], specifying exercises based on muscle contraction type may be less important than\npatient education about mechanical loading and the prescription of exercises according to\nwhat the patient is most likely to perform [36]. Adherence rates and patient satisfaction\nwith mixed protocols appear to be at least as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "text": "increases\nin tendon stiffness, large increases in modulus, and no clear change in CSA. The authors\nalso found that high‐strain protocols (~5%) elicited significantly larger increases in tendon\nstiffness and modulus when compared to low‐strain protoc", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0007_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0a28c2b7fcf9e696", "text_span_hash": "0a28c2b7fcf9e696", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "compared to low‐strain protocols (~3%).\nIndividual variability also plays an important role when prescribing load intensities\nto elicit specific strain values. In athletes, Achilles tendon strain during MVC tests vary\nsubstantially [96], highlightin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exercise protocols may be a viable option for treating tendinopathies [138], though con‐\nflicting evidence exists specifically for their use in managing AT [139,140]. With the mech‐\nanism of therapeutic action of mechanical loading on AT still unresolved [36–39] and\ngiven a lack of evidence favoring one contraction type over another [141–143], it does not\nseem justified to exclusively prescribe eccentric exercises for AT.\nAs opposed to universally prescribing eccentric exercises for tendinopathy, Millar et\nal. [36] suggest that clinicians should instead focus on conveying the principles of tendon\nloading and individually tailor the program to promote patient engagement with and con‐\nsequently the success of the program. Importantly, individuals with AT have highlighted\nthe burden of therapeutic exercise, particularly those prescriptions incorporating daily or\ntwice daily exercises [144]. With the biopsychosocial impact being a core tenet of AT [144–\n146], and coupled with patient perceptions that passive treatments (e.g., massage, dry\nneedling, ultrasound) are more efficacious than therapeutic exercises for managing AT\n[145], specifying exercises based on muscle contraction type may be less important than\npatient education about mechanical loading and the prescription of exercises according to\nwhat the patient is most likely to perform [36]. Adherence rates and patient satisfaction\nwith mixed protocols appear to be at least as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "text": "compared to low‐strain protocols (~3%).\nIndividual variability also plays an important role when prescribing load intensities\nto elicit specific strain values. In athletes, Achilles tendon strain during MVC tests vary\nsubstantially [96], highlightin", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
