low for an elevated exercise inten-\nsity and metabolic heat production dur-\ning self-paced exercise (49). In concert,\nthese adaptations reduce thermoregula-\ntory strain and improve physical perfor-\nHeat acclimation procedures devel-\noped in occupational settings trans-\nlated easily to athletes preparing to\ncompete in hot-weather races, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "eat\nwill allow for an elevated exercise inten-\nsity and metabolic heat production dur-\ning self-paced exercise (49). In concert,\nthese adaptations reduce thermoregula-\ntory strain and improve physical perfor-\nHeat acclimation procedures devel-\noped i", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
