ess of the AT is conducive to enhancing the transmission of muscle strength,\nthereby improving the running economy. Future research is required to explore more\ntraining methods to improve the biomechanical adaptability of the AT from the perspec-\ntive of mechanism and multiple influencing factors and reduce the risk of AT-related in-\nAutho", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "orimer, A.V.; Hume, P.A. Stiffness as a risk factor for Achilles tendon injury in running athletes. Sports Med. 2016, 46, 1921–\nPinto, M.S.; Sánchez, C.; Martins, N.; Menegaldo, L.L.; Pompeu, F.; de Oliveira, L.F. Effect of Achilles tendon mechanics", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0009_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b542f91f6d289abb", "text_span_hash": "b542f91f6d289abb", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of Achilles tendon mechanics on the\nrunning economy of elite endurance athletes. Int. J. Sports Med. 2021, 42, 1128–1136, doi:10.1055/a-1403-2606.\nKharazi, M.; Bohm, S.; Theodorakis, C.; Mersmann, F.; Arampatzis, A. Quantifying mechanical loading an", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "pared to traditionally shod runners (RFS), which seems more in accordance with the un-\nderstanding of mechanical adaptation of the tendon to different AT loading patterns\namong runners with different FSP [81]. This inspired us that multiple exercise and training\nmodalities combined with a step-by-step approach could lead to AT biomechanical adap-\ntations, so may do minimalist running.\nFirstly, high compliance of the AT was observed after the acute exercise intervention.\nMoreover, there are gender-specific effects in response to the acute loading. Specifically,\nthe stiffness and Young’s modulus of the AT show significantly lower in women com-\npared to men, which may be an important cause of fewer AT injuries in women. Secondly,\nperiodic plyometric training can improve the morphological adaptability of the AT, but\nthe exercise type, intensity, and duration are the three key factors that restrict the effec-\ntiveness of training. Finally, habitual running can effectively improve the mechanical\nproperties of the AT (e.g., greater stiffness, CSA, and Young’s modulus). In particular, the\nhigh stiffness of the AT is conducive to enhancing the transmission of muscle strength,\nthereby improving the running economy. Future research is required to explore more\ntraining methods to improve the biomechanical adaptability of the AT from the perspec-\ntive of mechanism and multiple influencing factors and reduce the risk of AT-related in-\nAutho", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "of Achilles tendon mechanics on the\nrunning economy of elite endurance athletes. Int. J. Sports Med. 2021, 42, 1128–1136, doi:10.1055/a-1403-2606.\nKharazi, M.; Bohm, S.; Theodorakis, C.; Mersmann, F.; Arampatzis, A. Quantifying mechanical loading an", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
