fness of the AT is conducive to enhancing the transmission of muscle strength,\nthereby improving the running economy. Future research is required to explore more\ntraining methods to improve the biomechanical adaptability of the AT from the perspec-\ntive of mechanism and multiple influencing factors and reduce the risk of AT-related in-\nAutho", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "medical_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "Brakke Holman, R. Using ultrasound measurement of the Achilles tendon in\nasymptomatic runners to assist in predicting tendinopathy. J. Ultrasound Med. 2020, 39, 491–496, doi:10.1002/jum.15125.\nLyght, M.; Nockerts, M.; Kernozek, T.W.; Ragan, R. Effect", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
