po-\nnent is usually (49) but not always (61)\nactionable information and highly moti-\nvational (49,61). Often, runners con-\nsciously and/or subconsciously deviate\nfrom their pacing strategy to remain\nwithin the group (80), consequentially\nadopting an unsustainable pace. Such\na decision may not be perceived as haz-\nardous due to large untapped energy\nresources (52) and negligible changes\nin ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "s\non this information is an essential deter-\nminant of pacing behavior (36).\nDeveloping mental skills (imagery, self-\ntalk, attentional focus, and goal setting)\nmay attenuate mental fatigue by modu-\neffort, attentional focus, and/or task\nElite marath", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
