{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f11f09e40d5318da", "text_span_hash": "f11f09e40d5318da", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (6