ormance at sea level. At high altitudes, vitamin E may\nimprove exercise performance [711]. Additional research is necessary to\ndetermine whether long-term supplementation may help athletes better\nImportant in blood clotting. There is also some evidence that it may affect\nbone met", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "text": "fects [719]. Additionally, it\nmay decrease homocysteine levels (a risk factor for heart disease) [720]. In\nwell-nourished and folate deficient-athletes, folic acid did not improve\nexercise performance [721].\nActs as a coenzyme for acetyl coenzyme A (", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "consensus_or_position_statement", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "evidence_weight": "strong", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": true}
