ale (n = 13) and female (n = 18) recreational runners took place. Focus groups were audio\nand video recorded, and transcribed verbatim. Transcripts were reflexively thematically ana-\nlysed. A critical friend approach was taken to data coding. Multiple methods of trustworthi-\nRunne", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "The Running Injury Continuum: A qualitative\nexamination of recreational runners’\ndescription and management of injury\nAisling LaceyID1,2*, Enda Whyte1,3, Sine´ad O’Keeffe1,3, Siobha´n O’Connor1,3,\nAoife Burke1,3, Kieran Moran1,2,3\n1 School of Health", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
