ury development, and ensure\npatients understand that it is normal to experience these responses (i.e. to experience anxiety\nor concern, especially with a lower level injury) [55]. Similarly, in helping athletes manage inju-\nries, coaches need to be aware of the importance of n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "text": "ior to presenting to them, and these attempts should be taken into consideration.\nFinally, with the emphasis placed by participants in the current study on the psychological\ndescription of each level of injury, it is crucial that HCP’s include biopsy", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0021_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "95bd3fdd88d72081", "text_span_hash": "95bd3fdd88d72081", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 21, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e their\npatients regarding the psychological aspect of the process of injury development, and ensure\npatients understand that it is normal to experience these responses (i.e. to experience anxiety\nor concern, especially with a lower level injury) [55", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Appropriate monitoring of RRIs.\nOur findings highlight the importance of a wider\nscope of monitoring RRIs, not just across all levels of injury, but across all categories of\ndescriptors. Firstly, HCPs should consider the potential importance of lower level injuries act-\ning as risk factors for higher level injuries (as discussed above), and appreciate that a runner\npresenting with a higher level injury may have had a preceding lower level injury, in order to\nbetter manage the whole continuum of injury. Secondly, when designing injury management\nstrategies, HCPs should understand that runners will likely have made attempts to self-manage\ntheir injury prior to presenting to them, and these attempts should be taken into consideration.\nFinally, with the emphasis placed by participants in the current study on the psychological\ndescription of each level of injury, it is crucial that HCP’s include biopsychosocial assessments\nwhen dealing with recreational runners, ensuring this psychological response is captured,\nmonitored and incorporated into management strategies. HCPs should also educate their\npatients regarding the psychological aspect of the process of injury development, and ensure\npatients understand that it is normal to experience these responses (i.e. to experience anxiety\nor concern, especially with a lower level injury) [55]. Similarly, in helping athletes manage inju-\nries, coaches need to be aware of the importance of n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "text": "e their\npatients regarding the psychological aspect of the process of injury development, and ensure\npatients understand that it is normal to experience these responses (i.e. to experience anxiety\nor concern, especially with a lower level injury) [55", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
