{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0001_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dcc82a7838475dd9", "text_span_hash": "dcc82a7838475dd9", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ective on the current state of subjective training load (TL) monitoring,\nand in particular sessional ratings of perceived exertion, for performance enhancement and injury prevention.\nSubjective measures may be able to reflect mental fatigue, effort,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The Current State of Subjective Training\nLoad Monitoring—a Practical Perspective\nJoseph O. C. Coyne1*, G. Gregory Haff1, Aaron J. Coutts2, Robert U. Newton1 and Sophia Nimphius1\nThis commentary delivers a practical perspective on the current state of subjective training load (TL) monitoring,\nand in particular sessional ratings of perceived exertion, for performance enhancement and injury prevention.\nSubjective measures may be able to reflect mental fatigue, effort, stress, and motivation. These factors appear to be\nimportant moderators of the relationship TL has with performance and injury, and they also seem to differ between\nopen and closed skill sports. As such, mental factors may affect the interaction between TL, performance, and injury\nin different sports. Further, modeling these interactions may be limited due to the assumption that an independent\nsignal can adequately account for the performance or injury outcomes. An independent signal model does not\naccurately reflect training environments where multiple stressors (e.g., mechanical, emotional, nutritional) impact\nadaptations. Common issues with using subjective TL monitoring, including a lack of differentiation between\nbiomechanical, physiological, and cognitive load, may be overcome by considering psychometric measurement\nbest practices, finer graded scales, and differential ratings of perceived exertion. Methods of calculating TL,\nincluding different acute and chro", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "ective on the current state of subjective training load (TL) monitoring,\nand in particular sessional ratings of perceived exertion, for performance enhancement and injury prevention.\nSubjective measures may be able to reflect mental fatigue, effort,", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
