tness” (positive) and “fatigue” (negative)\nfunctions can be quantified with internal or external TL\nvariables as training impulse [1, 5]. The genesis of this\nbasic TL model stems from the work of Bannister [5].\nA recent simplified extension of this work has been the\ndevelopment of the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "of TL-I\nand TL-P [19, 20, 33, 34], non-physical mental factors\nlike mental fatigue, effort, stress, and motivation have\nreceived comparatively little attention to physical fac-\ntors. Mental fatigue is a psychobiological state caused\nby demanding cogn", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
