centric exercise. This sys­\ntematic review supported the use of eccentric exercise for\nmidportion Achilles tendinopathy.\nBeyer et al14 found similar outcomes for a heavy-\nload, slow-speed exercise and an eccentric training\nprotocol. The heavy-load, slow-speed exercise pro­\ntocol included ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": ", –27.0 [–35.6, –18.0]; heavy, slow resistance\ngroup, –34.0 [–41.8, –26.5]), decreased anterior-to-posterior\ntendon width, and decreased Doppler signal. Although at 52\nweeks patients in both groups continued to have pain with\nrunning (mean VAS runnin", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "clinical_practice_guideline", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "evidence_weight": "strong", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": true}
