{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0044_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e115bad6330336f0", "text_span_hash": "e115bad6330336f0", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 44, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "miological data showing higher URTI incidences\nin athletes with either higher weekly training volumes\n(see Table 6) [36]. In fact, several risk factors have been\ndescribed that may promote URTI after a marathon race\nsuch as depression or anxiety [299", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nof compression socks in these athletes and among ath-\nletes with increased risk for thromboembolism should be\nMarathon running is associated with changes in the\nhematological system, especially changes in biomarkers.\nNonetheless, regular physical activity such as marathon\nrunning has benefits for the hematologic system such\nas reduced risk of thrombosis. Conclusively, marathon\ntraining and running are normally safe and beneficial in\nthe context of the hematological system.\nThe immune system plays a central role to protect the\nbody from pathogens and is of great importance in\nrestoring function. Marathon running puts immense\nstress on the immune system and its function [36]. Over\nthe past decades, the role of the immune system in asso-\nciation with endurance exercise enjoyed much research\ninterest. It became clear that physical activity strongly\ninfluences the immune system in various functions, both\npositively and negatively [17]. We identified 41 stud-\nies examining the beneficial effects as well as patho-\nlogical changes of marathon training and running on\nthe immune system. Table 6 lists the main findings and\nresults of the said studies.\nBiomarkers of Inflammation\nIn addition to the clear benefits of marathon training on\nthe immune system, high-intensity endurance exercise\nalso carries risks for immune function. After a marathon\nrace, various adverse effects on the immune", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "miological data showing higher URTI incidences\nin athletes with either higher weekly training volumes\n(see Table 6) [36]. In fact, several risk factors have been\ndescribed that may promote URTI after a marathon race\nsuch as depression or anxiety [299", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
