ount of research devoted to\nmiddle-distance training [e.g., 3–17], it is reasonable to\nargue that the developments in these disciplines have not\nbeen driven by sport scientists [18]. Publicly available\n“recipe books” and training diaries based upon the practi-\ncal experience and intuition of world-leading athlete", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "letes such as Paavo Nurmi, Gunder Hägg, Rudolf Harbig\nand Roger Bannister. The progression of female middle-\ndistance running performances was initially slower than\nthat observed for men [1], but this was due to social, not\nbiological constraints. B", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "evidence_weight": "moderate", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": false}
