urrent signs and symptoms of PFP.\nDecreased Hip Force Production\nPeople with PFP have weakness of the hip abductors, exten­\nsors, and external rotators.194,241,286 Rate of force development\nof the hip abductors and extensors is also reduced in people\nwith PFP.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety", "training_protocol"], "text": "duction.\nThere is a strong possibility that hip weakness is a result of\nPFP, and not a direct cause of PFP.245\nAltered biomechanics are commonly observed during func­\ntional movements in people with PFP. People with PFP may\nwalk, run,89,238 and negot", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "clinical_practice_guideline", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "evidence_weight": "strong", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": true}
