bia University on 09/01/19. For personal use only.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "text": "Risk Factors: Level of Evidence*\nApibantaweesakul 2017\nde Moura Campos Carvalho-e-Silva et al 2016\nDe Oliveira Silva et al 2019\nLankhorst et al 2013\nLankhorst et al 2016\nMaclachlan et al 2017\nMcMoreland et al 2011\nPappas and Wong-Tom 2012\nSteinberg", "language": "en", "quality_tier": "clinical_practice_guideline", "domain_pack": "injury_prevention_and_rehab", "review_status": "auto_classified", "needs_review": false, "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "evidence_weight": "strong", "auto_classification_basis": "quality_tier", "can_write_core": true}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0001_biomech_overstriding", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_heiderscheit_2011.url", "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1249/MSS.0b013e3181ebedf4", "source_label": "Heiderscheit et al. 2011 Effects of Step Rate Manipulation on Joint Mechanics", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "biomechanics_injury_prevention", "text": "Overstriding — landing with the foot significantly ahead of the center of mass — is the most common biomechanical error contributing to running injuries. It increases braking impulse, vertical loading rate, and patellofemoral stress. A heel strike with the foot extended more than 10–15 cm ahead of the hip at initial contact is considered overstriding. Running at higher cadence (170+ steps/min) naturally reduces overstriding.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["overstriding", "braking force", "foot ahead of COM", "overstriding injury", "landing mechanics"], "paragraph_index": 1, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0002_biomech_cadence_optimal", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_heiderscheit_2011.url", "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1249/MSS.0b013e3181ebedf4", "source_label": "Heiderscheit et al. 2011 Effects of Step Rate Manipulation on Joint Mechanics", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "biomechanics_injury_prevention", "text": "Increasing step rate (cadence) by 5–10% reduces peak hip and knee joint loads during running without altering speed. At most running speeds, a cadence of 170–180 steps/min (85–90 per foot per minute) minimizes overstriding and reduces impact forces. Runners with cadence below 160 steps/min at their easy pace have elevated injury risk. Use a metronome during easy runs to practice target cadence gradually.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["cadence 170-180", "step rate", "10% increase", "cadence injury prevention", "metronome training"], "paragraph_index": 2, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0003_biomech_hip_strength", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_blagrove_2018.url", "source_url": "Sports Medicine 2018", "source_label": "Blagrove et al. 2018 Effects of Strength Training on Physiological Running Economy", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "biomechanics_injury_prevention", "text": "Hip abductor and external rotator weakness is strongly associated with patellofemoral pain syndrome (PFPS), IT band syndrome, and tibial stress fractures in runners. The gluteus medius controls contralateral pelvic drop (Trendelenburg gait) during single-leg stance. Runners with >5° pelvic drop on single-leg squat testing are at elevated injury risk. Hip strengthening programs reduce running injury incidence by 30–40% in prospective studies.", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["hip abductor strength", "gluteus medius", "Trendelenburg gait", "pelvic drop", "PFPS hip weakness"], "paragraph_index": 3, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0004_biomech_vertical_oscillation", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_napier_2020.url", "source_url": "JOSPT 2020", "source_label": "Napier et al. 2020 Gait Modification and Overuse Running Injury", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "biomechanics_injury_prevention", "text": "Excessive vertical oscillation (bouncing) wastes energy and increases musculoskeletal loading. Optimal vertical oscillation for efficient running is 6–8 cm per stride. More than 10 cm of vertical oscillation indicates wasted energy that should be directed forward. Cues: \"run quietly,\" \"minimize the sound of your footstrike,\" or \"run as if the floor is hot\" reduce vertical oscillation in untrained runners.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["vertical oscillation", "bouncing running", "6-8 cm range", "wasted energy", "quiet running cue"], "paragraph_index": 4, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0005_biomech_trunk_stability", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_blagrove_2018.url", "source_url": "Sports Medicine 2018", "source_label": "Blagrove et al. 2018 Effects of Strength Training on Physiological Running Economy", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "biomechanics_injury_prevention", "text": "Core and trunk stability directly affects running economy and injury risk. Excessive lateral trunk lean, forward trunk flexion, or contralateral pelvic drop during running increase loading on the knee and lower limb. Core strengthening exercises (planks, side bridges, pallof press, hip thrusts) combined with single-leg exercises reduce trunk sway in runners. Running-specific core exercises should be practiced at race pace to transfer to running mechanics.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["core stability running", "trunk lean", "lateral trunk sway", "planks running", "single-leg exercises"], "paragraph_index": 5, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0006_biomech_arm_swing", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_roggio_2022.url", "source_url": "Frontiers 2022", "source_label": "Roggio & Musumeci 2022 Factors Affecting Training Recreational Runners", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "biomechanics_injury_prevention", "text": "Arm swing drives contralateral leg mechanics through the thoracolumbar fascial system. Optimal running arm position: elbows at 90°, hands relaxed (loosely cupped, not clenched), swinging forward-backward in the sagittal plane rather than crossing the midline. Excessive arm crossing creates trunk rotation and increases energy cost. Hands should not rise above shoulder height or drop below hip level during the swing.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["arm swing running", "elbow 90 degrees", "arm crossing midline", "trunk rotation arm", "hand position running"], "paragraph_index": 6, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0007_biomech_foot_strike", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_diebal_2011.url", "source_url": "JOSPT 2011", "source_label": "Diebal et al. 2011 Forefoot Running Improves Pain and Disability", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "biomechanics_injury_prevention", "text": "Foot strike pattern (heel, midfoot, forefoot) is not the primary injury risk factor — landing position relative to the center of mass is more important. Neither heel striking nor forefoot striking is inherently safer. A midfoot strike pattern with a low angle of attack reduces peak vertical loading rate but requires adequate calf/Achilles strength. Abrupt transitions to forefoot running increase Achilles tendon and calf injury risk.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["foot strike pattern", "heel strike injury", "midfoot forefoot", "landing angle", "Achilles forefoot risk"], "paragraph_index": 7, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0008_biomech_running_cadence_assessment", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_heiderscheit_2011.url", "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1249/MSS.0b013e3181ebedf4", "source_label": "Heiderscheit et al. 2011 Effects of Step Rate Manipulation on Joint Mechanics", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "biomechanics_injury_prevention", "text": "Assessing running cadence: Count steps for 30 seconds at easy pace and multiply by 4 (steps/min). Record cadence at easy, moderate, and race pace — cadence should increase with speed. If easy-pace cadence is below 160/min, a 5–10% increase over 6–8 weeks through metronome training or GPS watch cadence alerts is appropriate. Avoid increasing cadence by more than 10% in the first 4 weeks to allow musculoskeletal adaptation.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["cadence assessment", "step counting", "cadence increase protocol", "GPS cadence", "cadence training"], "paragraph_index": 8, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0010_load_acwr_safe_zone", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_gabbett_2016.url", "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2015-095788", "source_label": "Gabbett 2016 Training-Injury Prevention Paradox", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "The Acute:Chronic Workload Ratio (ACWR) quantifies training load balance. Acute load = training load in the past 7 days; Chronic load = rolling average over past 28 days. A ratio of 0.8–1.3 represents the \"sweet spot\" associated with lowest injury risk and highest performance. Ratios above 1.5 significantly increase injury risk. Apply to weekly mileage, time-in-zone, or session RPE × duration.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["ACWR", "acute chronic ratio", "0.8-1.3 safe zone", "workload injury risk", "training load ratio"], "paragraph_index": 10, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0011_load_10_percent_rule", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_nielsen_2014.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2014", "source_label": "Nielsen et al. 2014 Excessive Progression Training Volume Injury", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "The 10% weekly mileage increase rule is widely cited but has limited direct evidence. Prospective studies suggest that the absolute weekly mileage increase matters more than the percentage — increases exceeding 30 km/week in absolute terms carry higher injury risk regardless of percentage change. Additionally, the 10% rule applies to the acute:chronic ratio, not just consecutive-week comparison. Runners near their personal mileage ceiling should limit increases to 5–8%.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["10% rule mileage", "weekly increase limit", "30 km absolute increase", "mileage progression", "safe increase training"], "paragraph_index": 11, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0012_load_rpe_session_load", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_gabbett_2016.url", "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2015-095788", "source_label": "Gabbett 2016 Training-Injury Prevention Paradox", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "Session RPE (sRPE) training load: Perceived exertion (CR-10 Borg scale) × session duration in minutes = session load in arbitrary units (AU). Tracking weekly and monthly sRPE load provides a simple internal-load metric that predicts injury better than GPS distance alone. A well-loaded marathon training week for intermediate runners is 1500–3000 AU. Sudden spikes above 1.3× the previous 4-week average increase injury risk.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["session RPE", "sRPE", "CR-10 Borg scale", "arbitrary units", "internal training load"], "paragraph_index": 12, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0013_load_hrv_recovery", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_blanch_gabbett_2016.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2016", "source_label": "Blanch & Gabbett 2016 Has the Athlete Trained Enough to Return to Play", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "Heart Rate Variability (HRV) measured each morning (before rising, using a pulse oximeter or wearable device) reflects parasympathetic recovery status. A >7% reduction from a 7-day rolling HRV baseline warrants training load reduction. HRV-guided training — replacing planned hard sessions with easy sessions when HRV is suppressed — results in superior performance and lower injury rates compared to fixed-prescription training in 8+ week studies.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["HRV training", "morning HRV", "7% reduction threshold", "HRV-guided training", "parasympathetic recovery"], "paragraph_index": 13, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0014_load_training_monotony", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_gabbett_2016.url", "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2015-095788", "source_label": "Gabbett 2016 Training-Injury Prevention Paradox", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "Training monotony — the ratio of mean daily training load to standard deviation of daily load — should ideally be below 2.0. High monotony indicates all training sessions are similar in intensity, providing insufficient recovery contrast. A well-periodized schedule alternates hard (high-load) and easy (low-load) days. Calculate weekly monotony: (mean weekly load / SD of daily loads). High monotony + high strain = highest injury risk combination.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["training monotony", "load variation", "hard easy days", "periodization load", "strain monotony"], "paragraph_index": 14, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0015_load_mileage_ceiling", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_taunton_2002.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2002", "source_label": "Taunton et al. 2002 Retrospective Case-Control Analysis of 2002 Running Injuries", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "Individual mileage ceiling — the maximum sustainable weekly distance without progressive injury accumulation — varies by running age, athlete history, and recovery capacity. Most amateur marathon runners perform best at 60–90 km/week peak training load. Exceeding this ceiling without sufficient preparation (3+ years of progressive build) increases cumulative fatigue fracture risk. Signs of approaching the ceiling: persistent soreness, HRV decline, pace decline at standard RPE.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["mileage ceiling", "60-90 km/week", "individual maximum", "cumulative fatigue", "running capacity limit"], "paragraph_index": 15, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0016_load_tapering_injury_risk", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_roggio_2022.url", "source_url": "Frontiers 2022", "source_label": "Roggio & Musumeci 2022 Factors Affecting Training Recreational Runners", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "The taper period (2–3 weeks before marathon) reduces total training volume by 20–40% while maintaining some intensity. During taper, previously minor niggles often become temporarily more noticeable as cumulative fatigue clears — this is a psychological phenomenon (taper madness), not necessarily an indication of injury. Resist increasing training during the taper phase. Most taper-phase symptoms resolve on race day.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["taper injury risk", "taper madness", "20-40% reduction", "taper niggle", "pre-race symptoms"], "paragraph_index": 16, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0017_load_return_after_illness", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_blanch_gabbett_2016.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2016", "source_label": "Blanch & Gabbett 2016 Has the Athlete Trained Enough to Return to Play", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "Returning to running after illness requires conservative load management. General guideline: for each day of complete rest due to illness, reduce the ACWR acute load for 2–3 days upon return. After a viral illness (flu, COVID), wait until 48–72 hours symptom-free before resuming easy running, and avoid high-intensity training for 1–2 weeks. Post-viral myocarditis risk warrants physician clearance after systemic viral illness before resuming high-intensity training.", "quality_tier": "clinical_practice_guideline", "prescription_permission": "referral_and_flag_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": false, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["return from illness", "illness training", "COVID running return", "post-viral running", "illness load reduction"], "paragraph_index": 17, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0018_load_back_to_back_runs", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_roggio_2022.url", "source_url": "Frontiers 2022", "source_label": "Roggio & Musumeci 2022 Factors Affecting Training Recreational Runners", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "Back-to-back long runs (Saturday + Sunday long runs or mid-week double) simulate cumulative marathon fatigue and develop fat utilization capacity. Safe protocol for intermediate runners: first run 28–32 km, second run 18–24 km at 60–90 sec/km slower than race pace. Requires 10+ years running base or exceptional recovery capacity. Load spike from back-to-back runs requires 10–14 days for full glycogen and connective tissue recovery.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["back-to-back long runs", "double long run", "cumulative fatigue training", "fat utilization", "back-to-back protocol"], "paragraph_index": 18, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0019_load_periodization_marathon", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_roggio_2022.url", "source_url": "Frontiers 2022", "source_label": "Roggio & Musumeci 2022 Factors Affecting Training Recreational Runners", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "A 16–24 week marathon training macrocycle typically follows a build-up structure: Base Phase (6–10 weeks, aerobic volume + easy running), Build Phase (6–8 weeks, introducing threshold and long intervals), Peak Phase (4–6 weeks, highest-volume + race-specific work), and Taper (2–3 weeks). Within each mesocycle, a 3:1 build:deload week ratio (three progressive weeks followed by one reduced-load recovery week) minimizes injury accumulation.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["marathon periodization", "16-24 week plan", "base build peak taper", "3:1 ratio", "mesocycle structure"], "paragraph_index": 19, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0020_prevention_strength_running", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_blagrove_2018.url", "source_url": "Sports Medicine 2018", "source_label": "Blagrove et al. 2018 Effects of Strength Training on Physiological Running Economy", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_practical", "text": "Strength training 2× per week reduces running injury incidence by 30–50% in systematic reviews. Key exercises for marathon runners: single-leg squats, deadlifts, step-ups, calf raises, hip thrusts, and lateral band walks. Progressive loading over 8–12 weeks before peak marathon training. Strength training should be periodized: heavier loads in base phase, maintenance loads during peak training phase.", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["strength training injury prevention", "30-50% reduction", "single-leg squat", "hip thrust runner", "strength periodization"], "paragraph_index": 20, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0021_prevention_surface_variety", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_van_der_worp_2015.url", "source_url": "PLOS ONE 2015", "source_label": "van der Worp et al. 2015 Injuries in Runners A Systematic Review", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_practical", "text": "Running exclusively on hard pavement increases cumulative impact loading compared to mixed surfaces. Including 30–40% of weekly mileage on softer surfaces (grass, dirt trails, synthetic tracks) reduces cumulative bone stress without affecting training specificity. Avoid sudden shifts to trail running — uneven terrain increases ankle sprain risk in road runners unfamiliar with technical terrain.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["surface variety", "grass running", "pavement injury", "trail running transition", "mixed surface"], "paragraph_index": 21, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0022_prevention_shoe_rotation", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_malisoux_2013.url", "source_url": "JOSPT 2013", "source_label": "Malisoux et al. 2013 Influence of Running Shoe Drop on Plantar Load", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_practical", "text": "Rotating between two pairs of running shoes reduces injury risk by approximately 39% (prospective cohort data). Mechanism: each shoe design loads different anatomical structures, reducing cumulative stress on any single tissue. Minimal vs. maximal shoe rotation is personal — the key is varying heel drop, cushioning density, and fit between pairs. Both shoes should be worn-in and tested in training before racing.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["shoe rotation", "39% injury reduction", "two shoe pairs", "heel drop variation", "running shoe strategy"], "paragraph_index": 22, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0023_prevention_cross_training", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_van_der_worp_2015.url", "source_url": "PLOS ONE 2015", "source_label": "van der Worp et al. 2015 Injuries in Runners A Systematic Review", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_practical", "text": "Cross-training (cycling, swimming, pool running, elliptical) maintains aerobic fitness while reducing musculoskeletal impact loading. Pool running is the most specific cross-training for runners in injury recovery, maintaining cardiovascular and movement pattern specificity without impact. A 30-minute pool run approximates 50 minutes of road running for cardiovascular load. Cycling maintains VO2max but does not preserve running economy equally.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["cross training injury", "pool running", "cycling aerobic maintenance", "elliptical", "impact alternative"], "paragraph_index": 23, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0024_prevention_warmup_protocol", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_roggio_2022.url", "source_url": "Frontiers 2022", "source_label": "Roggio & Musumeci 2022 Factors Affecting Training Recreational Runners", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_practical", "text": "Dynamic warm-up before running reduces acute injury risk and improves initial running economy. Recommended 5–10 minute pre-run warm-up: leg swings (sagittal and frontal, 10× each leg), hip circles (10×), walking lunges (10 meters), A-skips (20 meters), ankle circles (10× each), and 2–3 accelerations building to easy running pace. Static stretching before running is not recommended — it transiently reduces muscle power output.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["dynamic warm-up", "pre-run warm-up", "leg swings", "A-skips", "no static stretching before run"], "paragraph_index": 24, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0025_prevention_cooldown_flexibility", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_roggio_2022.url", "source_url": "Frontiers 2022", "source_label": "Roggio & Musumeci 2022 Factors Affecting Training Recreational Runners", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_practical", "text": "Post-run cool-down and flexibility work: After 5 minutes of walking, static stretching of major running muscles (hip flexors, hamstrings, calf/Achilles, quadriceps) with 30-second holds reduces delayed soreness and maintains flexibility over time. Foam rolling of IT band, calves, and thoracic spine post-run may reduce muscle soreness. Focus flexibility work on areas of documented tightness — global stretching is less targeted and less efficient.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["cool-down", "post-run stretching", "30-second holds", "foam rolling", "hip flexor calf stretch"], "paragraph_index": 25, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0026_prevention_sleep_injury", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_van_der_worp_2015.url", "source_url": "PLOS ONE 2015", "source_label": "van der Worp et al. 2015 Injuries in Runners A Systematic Review", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_practical", "text": "Sleep is the most critical recovery and injury prevention intervention available to runners. Less than 7 hours of sleep per night increases injury risk by approximately 1.7× and impairs reaction time, glycogen synthesis, and musculoskeletal repair. Athletes sleeping 8–9 hours show significantly lower injury rates. Recovery interventions like ice baths and compression are far less effective than optimizing sleep duration and quality.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["sleep injury prevention", "7 hours minimum", "sleep injury risk 1.7x", "recovery sleep", "sleep over ice bath"], "paragraph_index": 26, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0030_early_recognition_pfps", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_kluitenberg_2015.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2015", "source_label": "Kluitenberg et al. 2015 What Are the Differences in Injury Proportions", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_early_recognition", "text": "Patellofemoral Pain Syndrome (PFPS, \"runner's knee\") early signs: anterior knee pain that worsens with stairs, squatting, or prolonged sitting. Pain typically begins after increasing weekly mileage or adding downhill running. Early intervention: reduce mileage by 20–30%, address hip weakness, and avoid painful activities. Continued running through increasing PFPS pain risks progression to chronic chondromalacia.", "quality_tier": "clinical_practice_guideline", "prescription_permission": "referral_and_flag_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": false, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["PFPS early signs", "runner's knee symptoms", "anterior knee pain stairs", "PFPS intervention", "knee pain running"], "paragraph_index": 30, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0031_early_recognition_achilles", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_kluitenberg_2015.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2015", "source_label": "Kluitenberg et al. 2015 What Are the Differences in Injury Proportions", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_early_recognition", "text": "Achilles tendinopathy early warning signs: morning stiffness resolving within 30 minutes of walking, localized tenderness 2–6 cm above the Achilles insertion (midsubstance), and pain that warms up then returns after exercise. The Royal London Hospital Test (pain on palpation of the tendon body) has 78% sensitivity. A positive test warrants immediate load reduction and initiation of progressive tendon loading protocol.", "quality_tier": "clinical_practice_guideline", "prescription_permission": "referral_and_flag_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": false, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["Achilles tendinopathy early", "morning stiffness", "midsubstance tendon", "Royal London Test", "Achilles prevention"], "paragraph_index": 31, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0032_early_recognition_shin_splint", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_kluitenberg_2015.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2015", "source_label": "Kluitenberg et al. 2015 What Are the Differences in Injury Proportions", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_early_recognition", "text": "Medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS, shin splints) presents as diffuse pain along the medial tibia (lower 2/3) that improves during running but worsens after. It is distinguished from tibial stress fracture (more localized pain, positive hop test) by imaging or clinical examination. Early MTSS: reduce mileage by 30–50%, avoid speed work, and address contributing factors (calf weakness, excessive pronation, sudden mileage increase).", "quality_tier": "clinical_practice_guideline", "prescription_permission": "referral_and_flag_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": false, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["shin splints MTSS", "medial tibial stress", "diffuse tibia pain", "hop test", "MTSS vs stress fracture"], "paragraph_index": 32, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0033_early_recognition_stress_fracture", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026", "source_file": "injury_curated_taunton_2002.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2002", "source_label": "Taunton et al. 2002 Retrospective Case-Control Analysis of 2002 Running Injuries", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_early_recognition", "text": "Tibial or metatarsal stress fracture warning signs: point-tender bone pain (reproducible with palpation of a specific small area), pain that worsens through the run rather than warming up, and visible swelling. Positive hop test (pain when hopping on one leg) has 70–80% sensitivity for tibial stress fractures. Suspected stress fracture requires imaging confirmation (MRI preferred over X-ray, which misses early cortical stress) before return to running.", "quality_tier": "clinical_practice_guideline", "prescription_permission": "referral_and_flag_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": false, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["stress fracture recognition", "point tender bone", "hop test tibial", "MRI stress fracture", "metatarsal stress fracture"], "paragraph_index": 33, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0040_load_monitoring_rpe_scale", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026_b2", "source_file": "injury_curated_gabbett_2016_b2.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2016", "source_label": "Gabbett 2016 Training-Injury Prevention Paradox", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "The CR-10 Borg scale for session RPE uses descriptors: 0 = nothing, 1 = very easy, 2 = easy, 3 = moderate, 4 = somewhat hard, 5 = hard, 7 = very hard, 10 = maximal effort. Session RPE is collected 30 minutes post-exercise when physiological arousal has cleared. For marathon training, long easy runs should score 2–3; threshold runs 5–6; intervals 7–8. Weekly load = sum of all sRPE × duration.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["CR-10 Borg scale", "RPE 30 minutes post", "sRPE guidelines", "long run RPE", "interval RPE"], "paragraph_index": 40, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0041_load_gps_data_metrics", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026_b2", "source_file": "injury_curated_roggio_2022_b2.url", "source_url": "Frontiers 2022", "source_label": "Roggio & Musumeci 2022 Factors Affecting Recreational Runners", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "GPS wearable data for injury monitoring: key metrics include distance per week, elevation gain (downhill running increases eccentric loading), training distribution (easy vs. hard), and cadence trends. Watch for: distance spikes >30% week-over-week, sudden elevation increases, and cadence drops (which indicate fatigue). GPS pace data alone underestimates actual physiological load — combine with HR or RPE for full load picture.", "quality_tier": "expert_review_narrative", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["GPS training metrics", "elevation injury", "weekly distance spike", "cadence GPS", "load monitoring wearable"], "paragraph_index": 41, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0042_load_injury_cluster_patterns", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026_b2", "source_file": "injury_curated_taunton_2002_b2.url", "source_url": "BJSM 2002", "source_label": "Taunton et al. 2002 Retrospective Running Injuries", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "training_load_management", "text": "Marathon training injuries cluster at predictable timepoints: 4–6 weeks into a training plan (musculoskeletal structures adapting to new load), 14–18 weeks (accumulated fatigue before taper), and in the first 2 weeks post-race (incomplete recovery). Runners with a history of specific injury (Achilles, PFPS, tibial stress fracture) have 2–4× higher risk of recurrence in the same structure. Modified load during these windows prevents the majority of training-associated injuries.", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["injury cluster timing", "4-6 weeks risk", "accumulated fatigue", "recurrence risk", "injury prevention timing"], "paragraph_index": 42, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0043_prevention_it_band", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026_b2", "source_file": "injury_curated_blagrove_2018_b2.url", "source_url": "Sports Medicine 2018", "source_label": "Blagrove et al. 2018 Strength Training Running Economy", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_specific", "text": "IT Band Syndrome (ITBS) prevention: hip abductor weakness is the primary modifiable risk factor. Strengthening protocol: clamshells, lateral band walks, single-leg hip bridges, and step-down exercises 3× per week reduces ITBS incidence in runners with identified weakness. Avoid sudden increases in downhill mileage and tight shoe fit at the lateral knee. Regular foam rolling of the hip abductors (not the IT band itself) reduces lateral hip tightness.", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["IT band syndrome prevention", "hip abductor ITBS", "clamshell exercise", "downhill ITBS", "foam rolling hip"], "paragraph_index": 43, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0044_prevention_plantar_fascia", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026_b2", "source_file": "injury_curated_van_der_worp_2015_b2.url", "source_url": "PLOS ONE 2015", "source_label": "van der Worp et al. 2015 Injuries in Runners Systematic Review", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_practical", "text": "Plantar fasciopathy prevention for marathon runners: Calf and intrinsic foot muscle strength training reduces plantar fascia loading. Towel scrunches, single-leg calf raises on a step (to mid-range only), and foot-core exercises (short foot exercise) address the intrinsic foot muscles. Runners with high-arched (cavus) or very flat (planus) feet benefit from custom or semi-custom orthotics to distribute plantar fascial load.", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["plantar fascia prevention", "calf raise step", "short foot exercise", "intrinsic foot strength", "orthotic plantar"], "paragraph_index": 44, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0045_prevention_runners_knee", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026_b2", "source_file": "injury_curated_blagrove_2018_b2.url", "source_url": "Sports Medicine 2018", "source_label": "Blagrove et al. 2018 Strength Training Running Economy", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_specific", "text": "Patellofemoral pain syndrome (PFPS) primary prevention: Running mileage increases of ≤10% per week, avoidance of downhill running during high-volume phases, and hip strengthening (gluteus medius focus) are the three most evidence-supported prevention strategies. A single-leg squat test (assessing pelvic drop and knee valgus) is a validated pre-season screening tool to identify high-risk runners before symptoms develop.", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["PFPS prevention", "patellofemoral", "knee valgus", "single-leg squat screening", "downhill PFPS"], "paragraph_index": 45, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0046_prevention_achilles_load", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026_b2", "source_file": "injury_curated_van_der_worp_2015_b2.url", "source_url": "PLOS ONE 2015", "source_label": "van der Worp et al. 2015 Injuries in Runners Systematic Review", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "injury_prevention_specific", "text": "Achilles tendon loading protocol for prevention: The Alfredson eccentric protocol (3 sets × 15 reps slow eccentric calf drops on a step, twice daily for 12 weeks) is the gold-standard tendon-loading program for both prevention and early-stage tendinopathy. Runners with Achilles stiffness should perform this protocol 8–12 weeks before a high-mileage training phase. Avoid stretching the Achilles into pain during acute tendinopathy phases.", "quality_tier": "clinical_practice_guideline", "prescription_permission": "can_write_core", "exclude_from_training_generation": false, "can_write_core": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "has_full_text": false, "source_status": "curated", "keywords_en": ["Achilles prevention protocol", "Alfredson eccentric", "3x15 eccentric", "tendon loading", "calf drop prevention"], "paragraph_index": 46, "page": null, "allowed_use": "rag_retrieval"}
{"chunk_id": "injury_safety_curated_0050_recovery_cold_water", "source_registry_id": "curated_injury_safety_2026_b2", "source_file": "injury_curated_roggio_2022_b2.url", "source_url": "Frontiers 2022", "source_label": "Roggio & Musumeci 2022 Factors Affecting Recreational Runners", "domain_terms": ["injury_safety"], "evidence_domain": "medical_safety", "expert_domain": "rehab_safety", "domain_pack": "injury_safety", "knowledge_layer": "curated", "section": "post_run_recovery", "text": "Cold water immersion (CWI, 10–